; A homogeneous array whose ELEMENT is a plain group WRAPPED in an anonymous array
; (`[* [coords]]`). The wrapper promotes the group to a standalone struct that writes its OWN array
; header. Each element is exactly ONE outer item (`1 * n`) and is read through the standalone
; `deserialize`.
;
; The element-read face and the serialize length both consult `RustType::is_basic` (plain-group AND
; not promoted), while the deserialize loop's branch formerly asked only `is_plain_group`. So the
; wrapped element was read standalone — correctly, one outer item — while the outer array's budget
; was charged the group's 2 fields, and the crate emitted bytes its own decoder rejected with
; `DefiniteLenMismatch`. Exit 0, compiles, round-trip red.
;
; Profile-independent: the defect and its fix are the same site under `--preserve-encodings`, which
; reads the length through `array_sz()` but keeps the same per-element accounting.
;
; The adjacent bare `[* coords]` spelling now owns a nominal flat-array codec: RFC 8610 repeats its
; group members flat without the nested array header a transparent collection alias would write.
; That semantic boundary lives in `homogeneous_plain_group_occurrences_use_flat_array_codec`.
; The MAP positions stay as controls: a table's length counts ENTRIES, not items, so a wrapped group
; in either the key or the value role costs one slot no matter what it expands to.
coords = (uint, uint)
wrapped_group_array = [inner: [* [coords]]]
wrapped_group_map_value = [inner: { * uint => [coords] }]
wrapped_group_map_key = [inner: { * [coords] => uint }]
